<<~ ahu slot=`#/body`>>

Terra-Unwired-Reviewer: Lares (Tasked Spirit). A ZERO-TRUST critical review, read-only, of production code that nothing in production calls. Repo: /home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine (the span head). Read code and canon (bags/**/*.mem) directly; write nothing. Trust no comment, no test, no canon claim until the code and the house's present rulings bear it out.
THE ITEMS (from a scout):
# `reconstructAndReadmit` (packages/lararium-node/src/recovery-keel.ts ~45) and `runReadmitEdge` (packages/lararium-keyhive/src/recovery-core.ts ~49): the recovery readmit — test-only.
# `rollEnrolments` (packages/lararium-mesh/src/persona-group-secret.ts ~227): sealed enrolments for devices at key rotation — test-only.
# `capTierShareDecision` (packages/lararium-mesh/src/federation-gate.ts ~550): declared-tier restrictions at the share gate — test-only.
# `makeContractSecretsRegistry` and `NO_CONTRACT_SECRETS_PLANE` (packages/lararium-node/src/plane-secrets.ts ~61, ~39): the secret-bearing plane registry — test-only.
# `KeyhiveIdentitySlot` (packages/lararium-keyhive/src/keyhive-identity-slot.ts ~158) and its orphan `deriveActorId` (~301): the identity-sharing capability barrier — production constructs none.
FOR EACH ITEM decide, with evidence: (a) LEGIT-OLD — a design the house's present rulings retired or superseded (cite the ruling, e.g. the road's #/rulings: mana triad retired; capability, not account; custody by capability never host; a shared act never makes a shared secret; the per-face random tag secret; the island capability record composing floor then lift; the recovery contact ruling K18), so it should burn, not wire; or (b) PONO-UNFINISHED — sound against every present ruling, missing only its last steps (name exactly which: the caller, the door, the verb, the composition point), so it should wire; or (c) PONO-BUT-WRONG-SHAPE — the intent stands but the code's shape breaks a present law (name the law and the shape it must take); or (d) UNSURE. Then a critical review of the code itself: correctness, fail-closed behavior, key handling (zeroing, no key across a port, no key at rest in the clear), refusals by name, clock use (no wall clock decides), test seams reachable from product, and whether its tests test production behavior or a fixture.
OUTPUT (final message, under 1000 words): per item: verdict (a/b/c/d) with cites, the critical findings (P1/P2/P3, file:line, MEASURED vs INFER), and — for b/c — the minimal wiring plan (where it plugs in, which reds must fail first). End with a recommended order.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/seat-law`>>

!! SEAT LAW (appended by tools/launch-codex.mjs to every brief; it binds this seat)
* A lane launches no Sol seat; the controller's one Sol read per train runs at the read floor of 0%.
* Stay inside the wall. Approval never escalates; a build seat has no network, loopback included; only a research seat takes `--search`. A step that needs more than the wall stops and names itself in `out.md`; the wall holds by refusal.
* Never commit and never run a git write. The lead commits each red, then each fix.
* Never build (`pnpm build`), never run vitest browser mode, never stand a vessel, socket or daemon. Typecheck (`tsc -p … --noEmit`) and run single test files that open no socket; mark any test that needs a socket "lead runs".
* A "stale build" from the dist guard stops the run; name it, and the lead builds.
* A read seat writes no file: its final message carries the hand-back. Any other seat writes `out.md` and every note in the seat directory alone. `-o` names `final.md`; stdin closes (`< /dev/null`); `TMPDIR` and `TMPPREFIX` sit inside the seat directory.
* Never run the battery; the lead holds its lock. Never read a credential file.
* Launch without `--ephemeral`: each seat keeps its rollout, so the spend tally counts it.
* The lead runs `pnpm build` in its worktree after every span merge and before each build seat; a verdict read off a stale dist counts as unread.

<<~/ahu>>

